# id: M80-a-new-message-type-joins-the-allow-list
# covering-test: TestRaftExitCriteria
# package: ./sim/hunt/
# expect: killed
# power-seeds: 400
# power-floor: 1
# power-ceiling: 200
# power-measured: 3 of 400, first at seed 99, under CURRENT (A7's shape) -- and seed 99 is one of the three seeds BUG-027 was actually observed on in the wild, which is as direct a corroboration as a floor gets. A floor of 1 in 400 is FRAGILE and is stated as such: the class is rare because it needs a follower forwarding a read inside the window between its own term bump and that term reaching disk. The earlier RESPONSE-only version measured 0 of 400, and that zero is a finding rather than a miss -- a leader's term is necessarily durable, so the response half is belt-and-braces and the request half is load-bearing. Same general form as M79's first version and a different axis: a mutant is a claim about where a defect lives, and a zero can mean the claim is aimed at the wrong ROLE -- leader rather than follower -- rather than at the wrong line. Four axes, one question, and no lane asks more than one of them: line (Track B's BM55), code position (make mutant-covered), role (this one), time (M79). DESIGN-A7 section 5e.2b
# mutates: BOTH read-index message types are added to send()'s allow-list and both call sites reverted to a plain send, so each leaves its node advertising a term that may not be durable. This is BUG-027 restored exactly -- but it is planted at the ALLOW-LIST rather than only at the call sites on purpose, because the structural fix changes what the human error looks like. Before the fix the error was forgetting to gate; after it, the only way in is adding a type to an enumerated list next to a comment saying the argument is the part that matters. So the mutant is the shape of the next occurrence. IT MUST INCLUDE THE REQUEST SIDE: a first attempt planted only MsgReadIndexResp and measured 0 of 400, and the reason is a finding rather than a miss -- the RESPONSE is sent by a leader, and a leader's term is necessarily durable (it could not have won its election otherwise), which is the same gated-by-consequence argument MsgTimeoutNow rests on. The reachable half is the REQUEST, forwarded by a FOLLOWER whose term bump may still be in flight, and all three violations observed in the wild say 'sent a read-index', not 'read-index-resp'. Killed by persist-before-reply, which holds every type but the two pre-vote types to Term <= durableTerm
